            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the dual blower door test (simultaneous - one fan up &amp; one fan down), we identified there are a few air leakage locations that exist:



** The location of the chimney that goes up from the lower level through the upper floor &amp; into the attic.



** The 5" chimney opening for the boiler &amp; the 5" combustion pipe that brings in air for the boiler (these will both be eliminated in conjunction with the high efficiency propane boiler recommended).



** The older doors &amp; windows.



** The attic hatch (can be sealed with a removable caulking such as Draft Attack)



** The wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls.



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I have modeled a model 10% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 42 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.2 GJ, and qualify for a $500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

Your house has 3 types of exterior walls:

      - Lower Level Rear Short Wall = 2.75' of uninsulated 8" concrete

      - Lower Level Walls (Front/Left &amp; Right) = stud wall that has had been renovated on the interior with additional insulation. Total estimated insulation of R24.

      - Upper Level are original 2x4 stud walls with R12 insulation



You are interested in updating the exterior of the house and this is a good time to add more wall insulation. I have modeled adding 3" (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior of all the stud walls (lower &amp; upper levels) and on the inside of the short section of bare concrete wall along the rear lower level.



We discussed the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For your house, I modeled adding R15 to all of the walls outlined above.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15 (adding 3" of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation), you could potentially save $ 1,751 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 50.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP),



Please note that completing a DHERP wall insulation upgrade is a requirement to qualify for some other rebates that you would otherwise not receive:

    - Boiler upgrade rebate is doubled from $ 750 to $ 1,500

    - An estimated air sealing rebate of $500

    - Window rebates of $250/eligible window (up to 12 windows max)

    - An estimated $3,000 Bonus rebate for reducing your overall energy use by 40% - 49% (for all upgrade savings reflected in this report).



You can look at the cost of the wall insulation upgrade vs. the estimated benefits. You would obtain the cost by obtaining quotes. The estimated benefits include:

    - annual energy savings of about $ 1,759

    - wall insulation rebate of $5,000

    - other incremental rebates of $6,750 ($750 extra for boiler, $500 air sealing, $2,500 for 10 windows &amp; $3,000 bonus)



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <windows>

You have a mixture of windows in your house; 7 older double pane wood windows, 3 double pane vinyl windows from the time when the lower level was renovated, and 3 newer triple pane vinyl windows in the kitchen "bumpout" section on the upper floor. In conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, this will represent a good time (as you could qualify for the $250 per window rebate) to update the 10 older wood &amp; vinyl windows to triple pane.



I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading these 10 current windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing these 10 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 492 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.3 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window ($2,500 total) in rebates through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your domestic hot water supply is provided by a loop from the boiler that heats your house. The current efficiency of this system, as well as an opportunity to increase efficiency (and reduce cost) is outlined within the previous "Heating System" section of this report.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a Buderus oil boiler (87% AFUE) that heats the main floor via a Hi-Velocity fancoil heat exchange unit. The upper floor is heated with in-floor radiant heat. The boiler originally had an integrated domestic hot water storage tank, however you mentioned that it had previously failed, so an external 151 litre water storage tank was installed .......... still heated by the Buderus boiler. The Buderus boiler is a single stage burning unit that does not modulate to recognize lower heating requirements of just heating the hot water, so the energy efficiency for the hot water component is lower at a 0.57 Energy Factor (EF). This boiler vents up a 5" chimney and there is a 5" open combustion air supply pipe to supply the Riello burner.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. This type of boiler will be modulating to adjust to changing heat &amp; hot water demand, and will eliminate the current 5" chimney and the 5" combustion air supply pipe. You would keep the current 151 litre hot water tank, and it would now be supplied by the new propane boiler. The energy efficiency for the hot water would increase to 0.82 EF.



Savings: By replacing your current boiler with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, that supplies both your heating and hot water needs, you could potentially save $ 1,878 ($1,668 heating &amp; $210 hot water) per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 42.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</HeatingSystem>
                <CoolingSystem>

Your house currently has a 284 litre electric hot water tank. With 6 occupants, you volume of hot water use will be quite high (showers, laundry, dishes, etc.) and the cost of electricity in Yellowknife is quite high. For you future planning, we talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 1,031 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</CoolingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs and fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM Pumps



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



In Yellowknife, houses connected to the municipal water supply will have a stainless steel circulation pump that operates continuously throughout the year. This pump is also eligible for a $175 ECM pump rebate. Based on current electricity rates in Yellowknife, the estimated savings for upgrading this pump can be $130 - $230 per year !



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has various components that contribute to ventilation:

        - there are 2 HRV's (one for each living unit)

        - there is a kitchen range hood fan in each unit that vents to the outside

        - there are 3 x bathroom fans that all vent to the outside (1 in lower unit &amp; 2 in upper unit)

        - there is a clothes dryer in each unit that exhausts to the exterior



As we identified during the evaluation, the HRV filters were completely plugged and needed to be cleaned. It is a good idea to clean these twice per year.



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seals well, use little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (2 x range hood fans, 3 x bathroom fans and both dryers), indicated a pressure drop of 7.1 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil boiler does atmospherically vent up a chimney, and could be susceptible to back-drafting combustion gases into your house.



If you upgrade the boiler to a sealed combustion unit (as outlined previously in this report), that type of boiler is not susceptible to back-drafting .......... and the current chimney &amp; combustion air supply pipe for the existing oil boiler can be eliminated.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors, although some of the smoke detectors looked to be near their "10-year" replacement. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre delivered

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



